.TH ARES_OPERATION 3 "16 November 2026"
.SH NAME
ares_operation_t, ares_query_dnsrec_cancellable, ares_send_dnsrec_cancellable,
ares_getaddrinfo_cancellable, ares_search_dnsrec_cancellable,
ares_operation_cancel, ares_operation_retain, ares_operation_release \-
submit and individually cancel retained DNS operations
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ares.h>

 typedef struct ares_operation ares_operation_t;

 ares_status_t ares_query_dnsrec_cancellable(
   ares_channel_t *channel, const char *name, ares_dns_class_t dnsclass,
   ares_dns_rec_type_t type, ares_callback_dnsrec callback, void *arg,
   ares_operation_t **operation);

 ares_status_t ares_send_dnsrec_cancellable(
   ares_channel_t *channel, const ares_dns_record_t *dnsrec,
   ares_callback_dnsrec callback, void *arg, ares_operation_t **operation);

 ares_status_t ares_getaddrinfo_cancellable(
   ares_channel_t *channel, const char *node, const char *service,
   const struct ares_addrinfo_hints *hints, ares_addrinfo_callback callback,
   void *arg, ares_operation_t **operation);

 ares_status_t ares_search_dnsrec_cancellable(
   ares_channel_t *channel, const ares_dns_record_t *dnsrec,
   ares_callback_dnsrec callback, void *arg, ares_operation_t **operation);

 ares_status_t ares_operation_cancel(ares_channel_t *channel,
                                     ares_operation_t *operation);
 void ares_operation_retain(ares_operation_t *operation);
 void ares_operation_release(ares_operation_t *operation);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The raw submissions perform the same DNS work and response conversion as
.BR ares_query_dnsrec (3)
and
.BR ares_send_dnsrec (3),
respectively, and return an opaque operation token. Existing submission APIs
are unchanged. This token is a logical operation identity, not a DNS wire ID.
.PP
.B ares_getaddrinfo_cancellable
performs the same address lookup as
.BR ares_getaddrinfo (3).
Its token owns the entire lookup, including both A and AAAA requests for
AF_UNSPEC, all search candidates, retries, failover, and UDP-to-TCP fallback.
Cancellation stops every owned child before delivering one terminal callback,
forbids further child submissions, and discards partial address results.
Successful address results transfer to the caller and must be freed with
.BR ares_freeaddrinfo (3).
.B ares_search_dnsrec_cancellable
performs the same logical search as
.BR ares_search_dnsrec (3),
owning every candidate and its transport work under one token.
.PP
On acceptance the function returns
.BR ARES_SUCCESS ,
publishes one caller-owned reference in
.IR *operation ,
and invokes exactly one terminal callback, including on synchronous errors or
cache hits. Publication precedes callback eligibility: the callback can read
the output token and reenter cancellation or retain/release. The callback may
run before submission returns. A non-success return means no acceptance, no
callback, and a NULL output token. The output pointer, channel, callback, and
query input must be non-NULL, except that address lookups accept NULL node,
service, or hints according to
.BR ares_getaddrinfo (3).
The raw DNS record is duplicated; it need not
remain alive after submission returns. Response records remain borrowed for
the duration of the callback, as with the existing APIs.
.PP
.B ares_operation_cancel
cancels only the supplied operation. If cancellation wins terminal selection,
the callback receives
.BR ARES_ECANCELLED .
Cancellation removes timeout/retry work and wholly unsent owned output frames,
including frames left on earlier connections by a retry. Unrelated operations
on the channel continue normally. A TCP frame that has already been partially
written may finish to preserve framing on a shared connection. Its residual
bytes do not refer to the cancelled query or callback state. Bytes already
handed to a socket write cannot be retracted; if cancellation reenters from
that write, its reported progress determines which residual bytes remain.
The remote server may still process such a request.
.PP
Cancellation and completion are serialized by the channel lock. A terminal
token, including one whose callback is still executing on another thread,
is a successful no-op on repeated cancellation. Cancellation does not wait
for an already selected callback to return. Callback arguments must remain
valid through callback return. No operation can be notified twice or revived
by a stale retained token, even if its former wire ID is reused.
.PP
Response matching retains the existing wire ID, question, and connection
checks. A delayed response for a cancelled operation may satisfy an identical
replacement operation after the same wire ID is reused on the same connection.
Cancellation adds no wire-ID quarantine or connection rotation.
.PP
The library holds an active token reference through terminal callback return.
.B ares_operation_release
only releases a reference; it never cancels active work. Tokens may be retained
and released independently of channel lifetime. After channel destruction,
retained terminal tokens can be retained, released, or cancelled (with a NULL
channel) without accessing the destroyed channel. Do not use a released token.
The library must remain initialized while tokens exist.
.PP
An active cancellation requires the submitting live channel. Passing a different
channel returns
.B ARES_EFORMERR
without affecting either channel. As with other channel APIs, the caller must
ensure an active channel call does not overlap destruction of that channel;
the channel lock does not keep a destroyed channel pointer alive. Channel
shutdown delivers
.B ARES_EDESTRUCTION
if destruction wins, or
.B ARES_ECANCELLED
if cancellation wins first. Submission to a channel already shutting down is
rejected before acceptance.
.PP
These functions are thread safe when c-ares is built with thread safety.
Without thread safety, callers must serialize operation reference changes and
channel calls themselves.
.SH RETURN VALUES
Submission returns
.B ARES_SUCCESS
on acceptance,
.B ARES_ENOMEM
if the token cannot be allocated,
.B ARES_EFORMERR
for missing arguments, or
.B ARES_EDESTRUCTION
if the channel is shutting down. Errors after acceptance are delivered only
through the callback.
The channel's finite 64-bit frame-identity space is never reused. If exhausted,
new wire queries fail with
.BR ARES_ENOMEM ;
existing queries and retained tokens remain valid.
.PP
Cancellation returns
.B ARES_SUCCESS
for active cancellation or a terminal no-op, or
.B ARES_EFORMERR
for a NULL token or an invalid active channel association. Retain/release
accept NULL as a no-op.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ares_query_dnsrec (3),
.BR ares_send_dnsrec (3),
.BR ares_getaddrinfo (3),
.BR ares_search_dnsrec (3),
.BR ares_cancel (3),
.BR ares_destroy (3),
.BR ares_threadsafety (3)
